{"records":[{"id":"t42mfkwds4","title":"of altered lipid species and pathways in luteolin- treated M1 macrophages","description":"Luteolin is a natural flavonoid derived from vegetables, fruits, and herbs and inhibits the inflammatory response in macrophages. However, there are few reports indicating that luteolin can inhibit inflammation by modulating lipid metabolism disorders. Non-targeted liquid chromatography-tandem mass spectrometry (LC-MS/MS)coupled with univariate and multivariate statistical analyses were employed to identify the differences of lipid class and molecular species in LPS plus IFN-γ activated RAW264.7 macrophages (M1 macrophages), accompanied with luteolin at non- cytotoxic doses. Differentially expressed lipids have been further analyzed using the MetaboAnalyst 5.0 software. As a result, 32 lipid classes were effectively down- regulated in M1 macrophages after treatment with luteolin. 85 lipid species showed significant differences in their levels between M1 macrophages group and luteolin- treated group,which involved in glycerophospholipid and sphingolipid metabolic pathways. Our study shows that luteolin altered lipid metabolism in M1 macrophages, which provide additional insight into the underlying mechanisms of luteolin on the inflammatory polarization of macrophages.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-06-15T20:00:16.219Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/t42mfkwds4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/t42mfkwds4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"BuChun","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Alternative Medicine"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"n5jchbtr34","title":"Data for Journal of Investigative Dermatology","description":"Cis-khellactone inhibited the proinflammatory macrophages via promoting autophagy to ameliorate imiquimod-induced psoriasis","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-02-20T15:38:16.194Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/n5jchbtr34","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/n5jchbtr34"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xudong","last_name":"Wu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Health Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"v7jysndj3m","title":"A GAPDH serotonylation system couples CD8+ T-cell glycolytic metabolism to antitumor immunity","description":"The neuroimmune signaling axis involving serotonin (5-hydroxytryptamine, 5-HT) has been well acknowledged. Apart from the canonical 5-HT-receptor signaling transduction pattern, 5-HT-involved post-translational serotonylation has recently been noted. However, whether and how serotonylation affects immune cell function remains poorly understood. Using copper click chemistry and an unbiased proteomic approach, glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase (GAPDH) was identified as the substrate for serotonylation in activated CD8+ T cells. Tissue transglutaminase 2 (TGM2) catalyzed the serotonylation reaction of GAPDH by covalently incorporating intracellular 5-HT into GAPDH Gln262 (Q262ser), resulting in enhanced cytoplasmic translocation and glycolytic activity. Glycolytic metabolism induced by GAPDH serotonylation supports the expression of the cytokine interferon γ (IFN-γ) and contributes to the antitumor ability of CD8+ T cells. Activated CD8+ T cells accumulate intracellular 5-HT for serotonylation through both cell-autonomous synthesis by tryptophan hydroxylase 1 (TPH1) and uptake from the extracellular compartment via serotonin transporter (SERT). Genetic ablation of TPH1 or SERT inhibits proliferation and IFN-γ production in CD8+ T cells, which subsequently impairs their antitumor immune activity. Monoamine oxidase A (MAOA) degrades intracellular 5-HT and acts as an intrinsic negative regulator of CD8+ T cells. Adoptive transfer of 5-HT-producing TPH1-overexpressing chimeric antigen receptor T (CAR-T) cells induced a robust antitumor response in mice. These findings reveal a GAPDH serotonylation system that promotes glycolytic metabolism and antitumor immune activity of CD8+ T cells.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-04T08:54:38.71Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/v7jysndj3m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/v7jysndj3m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xu","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhi-Gang","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":"0000-0001-8965-223X"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Tumor","Serotonin","Immunity","Cytotoxic T-Cell"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"58ckk6rzr5","title":"Dataset: The integrated stress response promotes macrophage inflammation and migration in autoimmune diabetes","description":"Type 1 diabetes (T1D) is an autoimmune disease characterized by the destruction of insulin producing pancreatic β-cells. Macrophages infiltrate islets early in T1D pathogenesis, preceding the influx of T- and B-lymphocytes. The integrated stress response (ISR), a cellular pathway activated during stress, coordinates adaptive changes in gene expression to maintain cell function and survival. To study the ISR in macrophages, bone-marrow derived macrophages were treated with a pharmacological inhibitor of the ISR (ISRIB) and polarized to a proinflammatory M1-like state. We found a reduction in the number of proinflammatory macrophages as well as a reduction in iNOS mRNA and protein levels with ISRIB treatment. RNA-sequencing revealed a reduction in pathways related to stress responses, including ER stress, reactive oxygen species (ROS) regulation, and autophagy as well as migration pathway genes. ISRIB treatment led to decreased macrophage migration after stimulation in vitro and decreased macrophages to the site of injury after tailfin cut in zebrafish in vivo. Pre-diabetic female non-obese diabetic (NOD) mice administered ISRIB demonstrated an overall reduction in the macrophage numbers in the pancreatic islets. Additionally, the insulitic area of pancreata from ISRIB treated NOD mice had increased PD-L1 levels. Our study provides new insights into ISR signaling in macrophages and demonstrates that the ISR might serve as a potential target for intervention in macrophages during early T1D pathogenesis.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-08-05T13:58:08.103Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/58ckk6rzr5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/58ckk6rzr5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sarah","last_name":"Tersey","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Analysis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3kw5zkgrb7","title":"Supplement table_IFN-gamma","description":"Supplement table for manuscript \"The Prognostic Role of Reactive Dermatoses in Anti-Interferon-γ Autoantibody Patients: A Retrospective Cohort Study \"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-09-29T12:31:48.972Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3kw5zkgrb7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3kw5zkgrb7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Tanat","last_name":"Eittidachachote","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunology","Dermatology","Infectious Disease"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"kwy6p9h667","title":"Data set for Alveolar Macrophages Undergo a Postnatal Transition from a Hyperphagocytic and Proinflammatory Profile to a Tolerogenic Profile","description":"This dataset supports the manuscript “Alveolar Macrophages Undergo a Postnatal Transition from a Hyperphagocytic and Proinflammatory Profile to a Tolerogenic Profile” (ISCIENCE-D-25-18795R1) submitted to Journal of iScience.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-03-02T13:18:24.521Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/kwy6p9h667","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/kwy6p9h667"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"航","last_name":"于","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Phagocytosis","Alveolar Macrophage"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6pc7vm5rpy","title":"Discordant effects of polyamine depletion by DENSpm and DFMO on β cell cytokine stress and diabetes outcomes in mice","description":"These data are the results of RNA sequencing studies comparing the effects of the drugs DFMO and DENSpm on MIN6 β cells, in both the presence and absence of a mixture of proinflammatory cytokines (IFN-γ, TNF-α, IL-1-β).  ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-02-08T07:54:29.226Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6pc7vm5rpy","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6pc7vm5rpy"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Raghavendra","last_name":"Mirmira","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/05aq6yn88","name":"Office of Extramural Research","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Cell Biology","Functional Genomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9c6zs82bxb","title":"Supplementary Materials for Development and External Evaluation of a Clinical Score for High Pretreatment Interferon-γ Release Assay Responses in Psoriasis: A Multicenter Study in China","description":"Supplementary Materials for Development and External Evaluation of a Clinical Score for High Pretreatment Interferon-γ Release Assay Responses in Psoriasis: A Multicenter Study in China","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-16T12:55:19.686Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9c6zs82bxb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9c6zs82bxb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"zhongrui","last_name":"xu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biologic Therapy","Psoriasis","Tuberculosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6c2sbrfycw","title":"SAMHD1 Attenuates Acute Inflammation by Maintaining Mitochondrial Function in Macrophages via Interaction with VDAC1","description":"Abstract:   Background: Over-activation of Toll-like receptor 4 (TLR4) is the key mechanism in Gram-negative bacterial infection-induced sepsis. SAM and HD domain-containing deoxynucleoside triphosphate triphosphohydrolase 1 (SAMHD1) inhibits multiple virus-es  , but whether it plays a role during bacterial invasion remains unelucidated. Methods: Monocyte-macrophage specific Samhd1   knockout (Samhd1−/−) mice and Samhd1−/− macrophage cell line RAW264.7 were constructed and used as research models to evaluate the role of SAMHD1 in TLR4-activated inflammation. Results: In vivo  , LPS-challenged Samhd1−/− mice showed higher serum inflammatory factors, accompanied with more severe inflammation infiltration and lower survival rate. In vitro, Samhd1−/− peritoneal macrophages had more activated TLR4 pathway upon LPS-stimulation, accompanied with mitochondrial depolarization and dysfunction and a higher tendency to be M1-polarized. These results could be rescued by overexpressing full-length wild-type SAMHD1 or its phospho-mimetic T634D mutant into Samhd1−/− RAW264.7 cells, whereas the mutants, dNTP hydrolase-function-deprived H238A and phos-pho-ablative T634A, did not exert the same effect. Lastly, co-IP and immunofluorescence assays confirmed that SAMHD1 interacted with an outer mitochondrial membrane-localized protein, voltage-dependent anion channel-1 (VDAC1). Conclusion: SAMHD1 inhibits TLR4-induced acute inflammation and M1 polarization of macrophages by interacting with VDAC1 and maintaining mitochondria function, which outlines a novel regulatory mechanism of TLR signaling upon LPS stimulation.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-04-28T13:04:28.973Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6c2sbrfycw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6c2sbrfycw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Bowen","last_name":"Xu","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/027s68j25","name":"Ministry of Science and Technology of the People's Republic of China","grant_id":"2018YFA0507401"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"82071762"}],"categories":["Inflammation","Sepsis","Mitochondrion","Macrophage"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xjxf6f89t3","title":"Data for: Isodeoxyelephantopin, a sesquiterpene lactone from Elephantopus scaber Linn., inhibits pro-inflammatory mediators’ production through both nuclear factor-κB and activator protein-1 pathways in LPS-activated macrophages","description":"The dataset includes all statistical analysis results for the Manuscript entitled \"Isodeoxyelephantopin, a sesquiterpene lactone from Elephantopus scaber Linn., inhibits pro-inflammatory mediators’ production through both nuclear factor-κB and activator protein-1 pathways in LPS-activated macrophages\". There are curently 27 files avaliable. The type of  all files is GraphPad Prism 7 XML Project (.pzfx). \nThe data were analyzed using GraphPad Prism Version 7.0. A Student’s t-test was used when only two groups were compared. When there were more than two groups, the intergroup significance was analyzed using one-way ANOVA with Tukey post hoc analysis. Differences were considered to be significant at P < 0.05.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-04-23T12:36:10.353Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xjxf6f89t3","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xjxf6f89t3"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yixin","last_name":"Han","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ximeng","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiaoyu","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuan","last_name":"Gao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ruijuan","last_name":"Qi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Runlan","last_name":"Cai","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yun","last_name":"Qi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Pharmacology","Airway Inflammation","Signal Transduction","Alveolar Macrophage"],"journal_issn":["15675769"]}],"total_records":134266,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":168,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":134266,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":133733,"restricted":64,"embargoed":469},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2304},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1351},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":645},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":470},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":435},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":420},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":409},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":374},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":307},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":273}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":131634},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1903},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":185},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":134},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":74},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":57},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":41},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":31},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":876},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":787},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":502},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":407},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":362}]}}